/-
# Rank of the Schur multiplier of a finite p-group (Kourovka Notebook Problem 4.67)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 4.67 (J. Wiegold): let G be a finite p-group;
show that the rank of the multiplicator M(G) of G is bounded in terms of the rank of G.
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.RepresentationTheory.Homological.GroupHomology.Basic
public import WantedExt.GroupTheory.PrimeOrderAutomorphismRankNilpotentKourovka13Problem58Wanted

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.SchurMultiplierRankPGroupKourovka4Problem67Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 4.67 (J.
Wiegold). UnsolvedMath record UM-00700274 (`KOU-4.67`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

The rank of a finite `p`-group is the largest number of generators needed by a subgroup: `G` has
rank at most `r` when every subgroup is generated by at most `r` elements, which is `RankLE` of the
Kourovka 13.58 entry, reused here (it quantifies over finitely generated subgroups, which in a
finite group are all subgroups); the rank of the finite abelian group `M(G)` is its least number of
generators. The multiplicator is `M(G) = H₂(G, ℤ)`, Mathlib's `groupHomology` of the trivial
representation `ℤ` in degree `2`. The bound `f` depends on the rank of `G` alone: it is chosen
before the prime, uniformly over all finite `p`-groups for all primes `p`.
-/

open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.PrimeOrderAutomorphismRankNilpotentKourovka13Problem58Wanted (RankLE)

/-- The Schur multiplier `M(G) = H₂(G, ℤ)` of `G`, as a `ℤ`-module. -/
noncomputable abbrev schurMultiplier (G : Type) [Group G] : ModuleCat ℤ :=
  groupHomology (Rep.trivial ℤ G ℤ) 2

/-- Kourovka 4.67 (J. Wiegold): the rank of the Schur multiplier of a finite `p`-group is bounded
in terms of the rank of the group alone: for some `f : ℕ → ℕ`, for every prime `p`, every finite
`p`-group `G` of rank at most `r` has `M(G)` generated by at most `f r` elements. -/
def schurMultiplierRankBounded : Prop :=
  ∃ f : ℕ → ℕ, ∀ (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime], ∀ (G : Type) [Group G] [Finite G], IsPGroup p G →
    ∀ r : ℕ, RankLE G r → ∃ s : Finset (schurMultiplier G), s.card ≤ f r ∧
      Submodule.span ℤ (s : Set (schurMultiplier G)) = ⊤

/--
Resolved true: the Kourovka Archive records 4.67 as done by Lubotzky and Mann (Powerful p-groups. I,
J. Algebra 105 (1987)): the rank of the Schur multiplier `M(G)` of a finite `p`-group `G` is bounded
by a function of the rank of `G`. The bound in the target depends on the rank alone, uniformly in
`p`. Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 4.67 (J.
Wiegold); resolution: A. Lubotzky and A. Mann, Powerful p-groups. I. Finite groups, J. Algebra 105
(1987), 484-505, Kourovka Archive, Problem 4.67: This was done (Lubotzky-Mann),
https://doi.org/10.1016/0021-8693(87)90211-0.
-/
public theorem_wanted schurMultiplierRankBounded_holds : schurMultiplierRankBounded

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.SchurMultiplierRankPGroupKourovka4Problem67Wanted
